WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:04.440
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.440 --> 00:00:08.670
The last few years have been an amazing ride
when it comes to research works for creating

00:00:08.670 --> 00:00:11.740
facial reenactments for real characters.

00:00:11.740 --> 00:00:16.940
Beyond just transferring our gestures to a
video footage of an existing talking head,

00:00:16.940 --> 00:00:22.740
controlling their gestures like video game
characters and full-body movement transfer

00:00:22.740 --> 00:00:24.330
are also a possibility.

00:00:24.330 --> 00:00:30.449
With WaveNet and its many variants, we can
also learn someone’s way of speaking, write

00:00:30.449 --> 00:00:36.020
a piece of text and make an audio waveform
where we can impersonate them using their

00:00:36.020 --> 00:00:37.020
own voice.

00:00:37.020 --> 00:00:40.420
So, what else is there to do in this domain?

00:00:40.420 --> 00:00:41.530
Are we done?

00:00:41.530 --> 00:00:43.570
No-no, not at all!

00:00:43.570 --> 00:00:48.379
Hold on to your papers, because with this
amazing new technique, what we can do is look

00:00:48.379 --> 00:00:54.649
at the transcript of a talking head video,
remove parts of it or add to it, just as we

00:00:54.649 --> 00:01:01.340
would edit any piece of text - and, this technique
produces both the audio and a matching video

00:01:01.340 --> 00:01:03.850
of this person uttering these words.

00:01:03.850 --> 00:01:19.640
Check this out.

00:01:19.640 --> 00:01:25.479
It works by looking through the video collecting
small sounds that can be used to piece together

00:01:25.479 --> 00:01:28.840
this new word that we’ve added to the transcript.

00:01:28.840 --> 00:01:33.189
The authors demonstrate this by adding the
word “fox” to the transcript.

00:01:33.189 --> 00:01:38.680
This can be pieced together by the “v”
which appears in the word “viper”, and

00:01:38.680 --> 00:01:43.040
taking “ox” as a part of another word
found in the footage.

00:01:43.040 --> 00:01:48.240
As a result, one can make the character say
“fox” even without hearing her uttering

00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:49.560
this word before.

00:01:49.560 --> 00:01:55.630
Then, we can look for not only the audio occurrences
for these sounds, but the video footage of

00:01:55.630 --> 00:02:01.010
how they are being said, and in the paper,
a technique is proposed to blend these video

00:02:01.010 --> 00:02:02.520
assets together.

00:02:02.520 --> 00:02:08.540
Finally, we can provide all this information
to a neural renderer that synthesizes a smooth

00:02:08.540 --> 00:02:10.940
video of this talking head.

00:02:10.940 --> 00:02:15.440
This is a beautiful architecture with lots
of contributions, so make sure to have a look

00:02:15.440 --> 00:02:18.580
at the paper in the description for more details.

00:02:18.580 --> 00:02:23.380
And of course, as it is not easy to measure
the quality of these results in a mathematical

00:02:23.380 --> 00:02:29.670
manner, a user study was made where they asked
some fellow humans which is the real footage,

00:02:29.670 --> 00:02:31.440
and which one was edited.

00:02:31.440 --> 00:02:37.500
You will see the footage edited by this algorithm
on the right.

00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:51.660
And, hm, it’s not easy to tell which one
is which, and it also shows in the numbers,

00:02:51.660 --> 00:02:57.240
which are not perfect, but they clearly show
that the fake video is very often confused

00:02:57.240 --> 00:02:58.510
with the real one.

00:02:58.510 --> 00:03:01.400
Did you find any artifacts there that give
the trick away?

00:03:01.400 --> 00:03:06.140
Perhaps the sentence was said a touch faster
than expected.

00:03:06.140 --> 00:03:07.140
Found anything else?

00:03:07.140 --> 00:03:08.920
Let me know in the comments below.

00:03:08.920 --> 00:03:13.650
The paper also contains tons of comparisons
against previous works.

00:03:13.650 --> 00:03:19.580
So in the last few years, the trend seems
clear: the bar is getting lower, it is getting

00:03:19.580 --> 00:03:25.290
easier and easier to produce these kinds of
videos, and it is getting harder and harder

00:03:25.290 --> 00:03:30.660
to catch them with our naked eyes, and now,
we can edit the transcript of what is being

00:03:30.660 --> 00:03:32.560
said, which is super convenient.

00:03:32.560 --> 00:03:38.700
I would like to note that AIs also exist that
can detect these edited videos with a high

00:03:38.700 --> 00:03:39.700
confidence.

00:03:39.700 --> 00:03:44.060
I put up the the ethical considerations of
the authors here, it is definitely worthy

00:03:44.060 --> 00:03:48.510
of your attention as it discusses how they
think about these techniques.

00:03:48.510 --> 00:03:54.310
The motivation for this work was mainly to
enhance digital storytelling by removing filler

00:03:54.310 --> 00:04:00.380
words, potentially flubbed phrases or retiming
sentences in talking head videos.

00:04:00.380 --> 00:04:05.300
There is much more to it, so make sure to
pause the video and read their full statement.

00:04:05.300 --> 00:04:09.210
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

